If both `fields` and legacy `select` are present, this block leaves `select` in the forwarded query (because deletion only happens when `fields == null`). To ensure the broker consistently receives canonical keys, delete `select` whenever it is present, while keeping `fields` as the source of truth.
```suggestion
                if (normalized.select != null) {
                    if (normalized.fields == null) {
                        normalized.fields = normalized.select;
                    }
```

If both `orderBy` and legacy `sort` are present, `sort` is forwarded unchanged due to the `orderBy == null` guard. Consider always deleting `sort` when present so only canonical keys are forwarded (with `orderBy` taking precedence).
```suggestion
                if (normalized.sort != null) {
                    if (normalized.orderBy == null) {
                        normalized.orderBy = normalized.sort;
                    }
```

If both `limit` and legacy `top` are present, `top` is forwarded unchanged due to the `limit == null` guard. Consider always deleting `top` when present so only canonical keys are forwarded (with `limit` taking precedence).
```suggestion
                if (normalized.top != null) {
                    if (normalized.limit == null) {
                        normalized.limit = normalized.top;
                    }
```

If both `offset` and legacy `skip` are present, `skip` is forwarded unchanged due to the `offset == null` guard. Consider always deleting `skip` when present so only canonical keys are forwarded (with `offset` taking precedence).
```suggestion
                if (normalized.skip != null) {
                    if (normalized.offset == null) {
                        normalized.offset = normalized.skip;
                    }
```

`QueryOptionsV2` includes `expand`, but `data.find()` never serializes any `expand` value into HTTP query params (and the V2→transport mapping does not include it). This makes the public API misleading. Either implement `expand` support for `find()` (likely as `expand=a,b`) or remove `expand` from `QueryOptionsV2`/docs for `find()` and point users to `data.query()` for expansions.
```suggestion
 *
 *  Note: `data.find()` does not support relation expansion. For `expand` /
 *  eager-loading semantics, use the more advanced `data.query()` API.
 */
export interface QueryOptionsV2 {
 /** Filter conditions (WHERE clause). Accepts MongoDB-style $op object or FilterCondition AST. */
 where?: Record<string, any> | unknown[];
 /** Fields to retrieve (SELECT clause). */
 fields?: string[];
 /** Sort definition (ORDER BY clause). */
 orderBy?: string | string[] | SortNode[];
 /** Maximum number of records to return (LIMIT). */
 limit?: number;
 /** Number of records to skip (OFFSET). */
 offset?: number;
 /** Aggregation functions. */
 aggregations?: AggregationNode[];
 /** Group by fields. */
 groupBy?: string[];
```
